    "* Diagnosis\n",
    "    Diagnosis of Alzheimer’s is a multi-step process in which doctors use several tools, including medical history, cognitive testing, brain imaging, and biomarker testing. Biomarkers such as amyloid-beta and tau can be measured by collecting cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) through a spinal tap. In addition, doctors may perform brain scans such as CT, MRI, and PET scans to support the diagnosis and help rule out other causes of cognitive impairment. These tests may also be repeated over time to monitor changes in cognitive function. If a person is diagnosed with Alzheimer’s, they may be referred to specialists such as geriatricians, geriatric psychiatrists, neurologists, or neuropsychologists.\n",
